# Director of Public Safety — Independent Brief

**Run:** Quiet-by-Design Standards for MWAA Terminals
**Chair:** Director of Public Safety, top-tier US hub airport (applied to MWAA / IAD & DCA)
**Discipline lens:** sworn airport police, ARFF and structural fire, and the 911/dispatch center under one chain of command.

I do not oppose the thesis. I read it twice and I still don't. What I want on the record — before the acousticians, PA vendors, and passenger-experience consultants get their hands on the design manual — is that terminal audio at IAD and DCA is not a comfort system. It is a life-safety system that has been re-labeled a comfort system by people who have never listened to it perform under load. A quiet-by-design standard that is correctly written will make my emergency communications *better*, not worse. A quiet-by-design standard written by people who confuse "fewer announcements" with "quieter" will get people hurt, and I will be the one on camera explaining why.

That is the frame. What follows is the evidence.

## 1. Key findings

1. **Terminal voice communication is regulated life-safety infrastructure before it is anything else.** NFPA 72 Chapter 24 governs the emergency voice/alarm communications system (EVACS) and the mass notification system (MNS). The intelligibility floor is a Speech Transmission Index of 0.50 or a Common Intelligibility Scale of 0.70, measured in each acoustically distinguishable space. That number does not move because a designer wants a calmer concourse. It moves the *ambient* number, because the STI target is a signal-to-noise problem, not a loudness problem. Quiet-by-design done properly lowers the noise floor and makes NFPA 72 compliance easier and cheaper. Quiet-by-design done poorly kills reverberation, hardens surfaces, or spreads incompatible zoning that fails commissioning and drives a retrofit two years after ribbon-cutting. [Source: NFPA 72, 2022 ed., Ch. 18 & Ch. 24].
2. **The FAA regulates my airfield response, not my terminal acoustics — but the airport emergency plan touches both.** 14 CFR Part 139.319 requires ARFF at IAD (Index E) and DCA (Index D or E depending on the largest air carrier aircraft in scheduled service) to reach the midpoint of the farthest runway within three minutes, with at least one required vehicle producing water in that time. 14 CFR Part 139.325 requires an FAA-approved Airport Emergency Plan covering aircraft incidents, bomb threats, structural fires, natural disasters, radiological incidents, sabotage, hijacking, and failure of power for movement-area lighting. The AEP is where the terminal PA lives from my perspective — it is one of my notification channels for a hijacking, an active shooter, or a bomb threat. If I lose it, I lose a controlled channel and I gain uncontrolled channels (rumor, social media, staff running through the concourse yelling). [Source: 14 CFR §§139.319, 139.325].
3. **Sworn airport police authority at MWAA is broad, but recruiting into it is slow.** MWAA operates the MWAA Police Department as a sworn agency with statewide police authority in Virginia under Va. Code § 5.1-158 and district-level authority at DCA via the MWAA compact and federal lease. Officers are DCJS-certified and complete academy plus airport-specific training including AOA driving, movement-area familiarization, TSA-directed sterile-area duties under 49 CFR Part 1542, and (for a subset) federal SIDA credentialing with fingerprint-based CHRC. Practical hiring cycle from posting to solo patrol is 12–18 months. That number does not compress no matter how urgent the vacancy. When the thesis introduces "staffed communication" as a redundant channel to replace PA output, the strategist should assume those staff hours cost more than they look and take longer to hire than they look. [Source: Va. Code Ann. § 5.1-158; 49 CFR Part 1542; MWAA Police recruiting materials].
4. **The PSAP question at MWAA is not settled by an org chart.** MWAA operates a Public Safety Communications Center that dispatches MWAA Police and Fire on-airport. But a passenger calling 911 from a Verizon or T-Mobile handset on a jet bridge does not necessarily route to me. Wireless 911 routing depends on cell-sector geolocation and, increasingly, device-based hybrid location (Apple HELO, Google ELS). A call from Concourse C at IAD may land in Loudoun County's ECC, or Fairfax County's, before it is transferred to MWAA. Under NG911 transition to i3, geospatial routing will improve — but "improve" over a decade of migration is not "solve." Any quiet-by-design standard that reduces PA load and shifts self-help toward "call 911" needs to have answered which PSAP takes the call, in what median seconds, and who does the warm handoff. [Source: FCC Public Safety and Homeland Security Bureau, wireless 911 rules 47 CFR §§20.18, 9.10; NENA i3 Standard NENA-STA-010.3b-2021].
5. **Mutual aid works in the drill and fails at 3 a.m. more often than airport executives admit.** MWAA has signed agreements with Loudoun, Fairfax, Arlington, and Alexandria fire and police, and with the National Airport Fire Department mutual-aid box alarm system. What actually shows up at 3 a.m. is a function of the calling agency's own load that night, the highway conditions between them and the AOA gate, and whether their crews have current Movement Area Driver Training. During the January 29, 2025 PSA 5342 / Army UH-60 midair at DCA, mutual-aid response from Arlington County, Alexandria, DC Fire and EMS, and the U.S. Park Police was rapid because the incident occurred in the Potomac, not because paper agreements worked — they worked because the responders had trained together. For a quiet-by-design standard, the relevant question is whether the shift of communicative burden from PA to "staffed and mutual-aid supported" channels holds up during a real IROP night with 40,000 stranded passengers. [Source: NTSB DCA25MA108 preliminary report, March 11, 2025].
6. **The Fort Lauderdale 2017 active-shooter incident is the single most important case study for the thesis.** On January 6, 2017 a shooter killed five and wounded six in the FLL Terminal 2 baggage claim. What made FLL a national teaching case was not the shooting — which was over in 80 seconds. It was the four subsequent hours: two false active-shooter reports on the sterile side, uncontrolled self-evacuation onto the AOA, passengers on the taxiways, radio interoperability failures across BSO, FLPD, and airport operations, and a public-address system that could not push a coherent instruction to the entire complex. The Broward Sheriff's Office After-Action Report identified PA and mass-notification failures as a primary lesson. Any quiet-by-design standard that pares back PA capability without hardening zoned emergency-override capacity is proposing to accept FLL's failure mode on purpose. [Source: Broward Sheriff's Office FLL Airport Active Shooter After-Action Report, February 2018].
7. **The FLL, LGA-2016, JFK-2016, and Newark false-active-shooter incidents share one architectural failure: undifferentiated PA zoning under emergency load.** In the August 2016 LGA and JFK "shots fired" panic events (both false), the emergency PA either could not localize an instruction to the affected concourse or could not overcome the ambient rumor cascade. Passengers self-evacuated onto ramps and runways at both airports. The Port Authority Office of the Inspector General reviewed the JFK response. Zoned, directional, high-intelligibility PA is the countermeasure. Quiet-by-design that specifies dense loudspeaker arrays with narrow throw and high STI actually solves this problem. Quiet-by-design that specifies "fewer speakers, softer overall" makes it worse. The standard has to be prescriptive on this distinction or it will get one of the two. [Source: Port Authority OIG report on JFK Aug 14, 2016 incident, Nov. 2016; PANYNJ post-incident review].
8. **The Atlanta ATL power outage of December 17, 2017 is the case for redundant, non-electrical wayfinding and staffed communication.** An eleven-hour underground fire disabled Georgia Power's primary and backup service to the terminal. Radios worked; cellular was overwhelmed; PA was dark for most of the outage in most of the complex; FIDS was down; jet bridges immobile. Delta cancelled ~1,400 flights. The lesson for a quiet-by-design standard: the redundant visual and digital channels that quiet-by-design leans on must have emergency power segregation that matches the PA's Class-A survivability, or the "redundancy" is nominal. [Source: Delta Air Lines press release Dec 17–18, 2017; ATL after-action summary, City of Atlanta Council briefing Jan 2018].

## 2. Evidence — with sources

### 2.1 The regulatory floor: NFPA 72 and Part 139

NFPA 72 (National Fire Alarm and Signaling Code), 2022 edition, Chapter 24, defines emergency voice/alarm communications systems (EVACS) and mass notification systems (MNS). Intelligibility is measured in Acoustically Distinguishable Spaces (ADS). Each ADS must achieve STI ≥ 0.50 (nominally "fair" intelligibility) or CIS ≥ 0.70, per Annex D. Fire alarm signal audibility per Chapter 18 is 15 dBA above average ambient, or 5 dBA above the maximum 60-second sound level, whichever is greater; where sleep spaces are absent and voice systems are used, the intelligibility test replaces the tone-audibility test in occupied areas. [Source: NFPA 72-2022 §18.4, §24.4, Annex D — cited as source_citation; standard is paywalled].

Airport certification under 14 CFR Part 139 is silent on terminal acoustics but explicit on ARFF response and airport emergency planning. §139.319(h) requires ARFF vehicles reach the midpoint of the most distant runway (or any point of a wider assigned response area) within 3 minutes from the time of alarm, with at least one required vehicle capable of applying agent within that time. §139.325 requires an FAA-approved Airport Emergency Plan and drills including a full-scale exercise every 36 months. [Source: https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-14/chapter-I/subchapter-G/part-139].

### 2.2 Occupied areas and employee noise

OSHA 29 CFR 1910.95 sets an 85 dBA action level (8-hour TWA) and 90 dBA PEL. Concourse ambient at large-hub gates in current MWAA operations is generally 65–75 dBA, spiking to 80–90 dBA at boarding and during structure-borne apron noise. Quiet-by-design lowers this exposure and reduces MWAA's employer-noise-program burden. That is a public-safety win for my personnel — Communications Center dispatchers, TSOs (not my staff, but on my property), gate agents, and airport police assigned to concourse posts. [Source: 29 CFR 1910.95; ACRP Synthesis 9, *Effects of Aircraft Noise: Research Update on Selected Topics*, 2008].

### 2.3 ADA and accessibility redundancy

ADA Title II and the 2010 ADA Standards for Accessible Design require visual notification appliances in occupied areas per NFPA 72 Chapter 18 candela ratings, and specifically require both audible and visual alarm notification in public and common-use areas. DOT Air Carrier Access Act 14 CFR Part 382 and the 2024 ACAA rulemakings on accessible communication push airline-side redundancy. A standard that reduces PA output must specify the visual/digital compensating capability with the same measurable rigor. [Source: 28 CFR Part 35; ADA Standards §215, §702; 14 CFR Part 382].

### 2.4 Case studies

- **Fort Lauderdale, Jan 6, 2017.** BSO After-Action Report (Feb 2018) documents PA and mass-notification failures during and after the terminal-2 shooting; secondary false active-shooter reports triggered uncontrolled AOA breaches. [Source: https://www.sheriff.org — Broward Sheriff's Office After-Action Review].
- **LaGuardia, Aug 14, 2016 & JFK Aug 14, 2016 (same night, false shots-fired panics).** Both airports experienced mass self-evacuation onto airfield areas; PA zoning and information vacuum cited by PANYNJ OIG. [Source: PANYNJ Office of Inspector General, JFK After-Action, Nov 2016].
- **Atlanta ATL power outage, Dec 17, 2017.** 11-hour outage disabled terminal PA, FIDS, and jet bridges. [Source: City of Atlanta briefing to Council, Jan 2018; NTSB not involved].
- **PSA 5342 / UH-60 midair near DCA, Jan 29, 2025.** Mutual aid response into the Potomac included Arlington County Fire, Alexandria Fire/EMS, DC FEMS, Fairfax County Fire/Rescue, U.S. Park Police Aviation, and MWAA Fire; the incident is instructive for the *coordination* work quiet-by-design shifts partly onto communications, not just for the tragedy itself. [Source: NTSB DCA25MA108; MWAA public statements, Jan-Feb 2025].
- **BWI Nov 2013 baggage-claim stampede after a report of a firearm** and multiple "unattended bag" concourse closures at IAD, DCA, and peers over 2018–2024 demonstrate the daily-operations version of the FLL failure mode: information vacuum → passenger panic → operational cost. [Source: BWI Fire/Rescue after-action; DOT/OIG audits on unattended bag responses at Category X airports].

### 2.5 PSAP and NG911

FCC rules on wireless 911 (47 CFR §20.18) require handset-based Phase II location; NG911 (NENA i3, NENA-STA-010.3b-2021) uses ECRF/LVF for geospatial routing but is deployed unevenly across Virginia, DC, and Maryland. MWAA Public Safety Communications Center is the primary PSAP for calls originated on the airport by hard-line and airport-network sources; wireless calls from within the terminal envelope route based on serving cell sector and may terminate at a county PSAP first. This is a documented weakness at every major US airport and is a real operational risk if the design standard reduces PA and expects "call 911" to fill the gap. [Source: NENA i3 Standard; FCC Sixth Report and Order on wireless 911, 2015].

### 2.6 Labor and capital

- **ARFF apparatus.** A Rosenbauer Panther 6x6 or Oshkosh Striker 6x6 (Index E vehicle) currently prices at approximately $1.6M–$2.1M per unit delivered, with a 24–30 month build lead time. IAD needs a fleet of at least three primary Index E vehicles plus reserve; a replacement program on a 15-year cycle is $10–15M per decade in vehicle capital alone, before hose, PPE, and station capital. [Source: recent GSA schedule pricing and city-council procurement records from peer hubs 2023–2025].
- **Police vehicles** on a 4–6 year cycle for pursuit-rated Interceptors, 6–8 for admin, plus SWAT, BearCat/CATV, K9, and marine assets at DCA on the Potomac.
- **P25 land-mobile radio.** MWAA operates on a Project 25 system with in-building DAS coverage in terminals; a full-fleet portable/mobile replacement runs $8k–$12k per portable and $4k–$8k per mobile. Radio infrastructure lifecycle is 12–15 years. [Source: DHS SAFECOM guidance; APCO P25 Compliance Assessment Program].
- **Recruitment.** IAFF Local 3217 (MWAA Fire) and MWAA Police union (FOP Lodge unaffiliated / IUPA depending on unit) collective bargaining cycles run 3–5 years. FLSA §7(k) applies to fire and police; the 28-day/171-hour cycle governs overtime accrual. Compressed shift schedules (24/72, 48/96) interact with §7(k) in ways that can create surprise overtime liability if the standard drives a staffing increase for terminal ambassadors classified as sworn. [Source: 29 U.S.C. §207(k); DOL Wage and Hour Division Fact Sheet #8].

## 3. The public safety case *for* the thesis

**The strongest argument for quiet-by-design from my chair is that it improves emergency communications.** The intelligibility problem in a large terminal is a signal-to-noise problem. Lower the ambient — through absorption, HVAC design, quieter jet-bridge equipment, gate-hold PA discipline, muted commercial TVs, and zoned rather than blanket announcements — and the STI number climbs without adding a single loudspeaker. My cost to comply with NFPA 72 goes down. My mass-notification system becomes more capable, not less. When I have to push an active-shooter shelter-in-place instruction to Concourse D at IAD, I want a passenger three meters from a gate-side speaker to understand it the first time. Quiet-by-design, correctly specified, is how I get that.

The second argument for is that quiet-by-design forces the operator to build the redundant visual, digital, and staffed channels that the AEP already assumes exist. Most airports write an AEP that names FIDS, mobile push notification, and staffed ambassadors as communication channels and then never verifies they function under load. A standard that requires those channels to meet measurable performance and to have emergency-power segregation is a standard that finally forces the AEP off the shelf.

The third argument is workforce. Lower ambient reduces employee noise exposure, reduces radio unintelligibility on the concourse, and reduces the cumulative cognitive load that shows up in my dispatcher-error rate and my officer citizen-complaint rate. These are second-order effects the ROI models will not capture, and they are real.

## 4. The public safety case *against* — or, more precisely, the case for scoping the thesis narrowly

**The strongest argument against a permissive quiet-by-design standard is that "quiet" and "intelligible" are not the same variable and will be confused by every consultant who reads the standard.** If MWAA writes "reduce announcements" or "reduce sound levels" as the top-line target, we will get value engineering that removes loudspeakers, deletes zones, and thins the DAS. Then the first bomb threat that requires a concourse-specific evacuation will discover the gap. This is exactly the FLL failure mode.

The second argument against is that the redundant channels that "quiet" leans on are more failure-prone than the PA. Cellular saturates in a crowd event. Mobile push requires an opt-in most passengers have not made. FIDS runs on a network that is not fire-alarm-survivable in most terminals. Staffed ambassadors are the first labor line cut in a budget squeeze. Every one of these has failed at a peer airport in the last five years. A design standard that formally shifts weight onto them without hardening them is trading a working system for a nominal one.

The third argument against is that a common MWAA standard applied to two very different airports will over-specify one and under-specify the other. DCA's Terminal 1 is a historic building with acoustic constraints and a passenger profile of frequent short-haul business travelers who can absorb information from FIDS. IAD's international arrivals concourses serve 10.5M international passengers annually with variable English proficiency, unfamiliarity with US airport conventions, and a high need for repeated, translated audio. A single standard that reads well at DCA will be dangerous at IAD's FIS.

The fourth argument against is capital sequencing. The July 29, 2026 Dulles $20B+ announcement means I will be re-baselining fire alarm, PA, mass notification, and DAS across a decade of construction. Locking a standard now that is prescriptive on technology and vendor rather than on performance will lose to product-cycle changes over that decade. The standard has to be performance-based and technology-agnostic — STI numbers, zone counts, survivability class, response times — not "install product X."

## 5. Specific facts a strategist can use

1. **NFPA 72 STI floor of 0.50 is the non-negotiable number.** Any quiet-by-design standard that does not open with this number as its central compliance metric is unserious. Corollary: the standard should require STI verification at commissioning in every ADS *and* re-verification after any tenant fit-out that changes the acoustic envelope.
2. **Part 139 §139.325 requires a full-scale AEP exercise every 36 months.** MWAA's next scheduled full-scale exercise is a live test bed for whatever communication architecture the standard specifies. Write the standard so its performance metrics *are* the exercise evaluation criteria — do not evaluate the exercise on a separate checklist.
3. **ARFF Index E apparatus lead time is 24–30 months and unit cost is $1.6M–$2.1M.** Any change in terminal geometry from the Dulles program that moves runway midpoints or extends response paths must be modeled against this replacement cycle before design is frozen.
4. **The FLL 2017 After-Action Report and the PANYNJ OIG JFK-2016 review are the two documents the strategist should cite by name.** Both directly implicate PA and mass-notification design in the escalation. Both are public.
5. **PSAP routing is the sleeper failure mode.** Ask, on the record, what percentage of wireless 911 calls originated inside IAD and DCA terminals in 2025 landed at MWAA's PSAP on first-ring vs. after transfer. If MWAA cannot answer, that is the finding. A quiet-by-design standard that expects passengers to "just call for help" without that number is designing on hope.
6. **Recruiting a sworn officer takes 12–18 months from posting to solo patrol, and hiring an ARFF firefighter with FAA-required Live Fire Training currency takes 9–12 months.** "Staffed communication" as a redundant channel is not a knob the operator can turn quickly.
7. **My professional judgment:** the correct posture for MWAA is to adopt a two-tier standard — a mandatory tier that fixes NFPA 72 intelligibility, MNS zoning, DAS survivability, ADA visual/digital redundancy, and PSAP routing as measurable, testable requirements applicable to both airports; and a context-dependent tier that lets IAD FIS and DCA Terminal 1 diverge on quiet-zone geometry, announcement scripting, and TV muting policy. The mandatory tier is the floor a public-safety chair can defend to the Board and the media the day after an incident. The optional tier is where the passenger-experience upside lives.

